      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, as a kid? Yeah, definitely. This isn't some new thing that just started when Uncle Arthur moved in, if that's what you're thinking. My mother used to joke that even in elementary school, I was the one teaching the class because I could never just sit in my seat. I was always up, wandering around, talking, cutting in before the teacher even finished giving directions. \n\nI remember Mrs. Gable in third grade—she had this huge beehive hairdo that must have taken a whole can of hairspray every morning. Seriously, you could smell the Aqua Net from the hallway. She used to make me sit right next to her desk, but even then, I'd be up sharpening my pencil five times an hour. Those old crank sharpeners, you know? The ones bolted to the wall that would chew your pencil down to a nub if you weren't careful. I used to love the smell of those cedar shavings. \n\nActually, I bought one of those vintage crank sharpeners for my classroom last year at a flea market. Paid twenty bucks for it. I thought the kids would get a kick out of it, maybe appreciate the tactile experience, you know? But they don't even use regular pencils anymore! It's all mechanical pencils with those tiny little leads that snap if you look at them wrong. I spend half my day sweeping up little bits of graphite from the floor. The custodian, Gary, he's always complaining about it. Gary's a good guy, though. He's been with the district for thirty years. He was telling me the other day about his pension plan, and honestly, I should have looked into the state retirement system earlier because the way things are going...\n\nBut yeah, the restlessness and getting sidetracked? That's just always been me. Long before adulthood, long before any of this. It's just how I've always been wired.",
